# Pull main before applying or numbering an engine overlay

This is a **must-do** rule (Josh, November 1).

Several sessions publish engine changes to main at the same time. Every
overlay step starts from current main:

1. **Before choosing an overlay version**, `git fetch origin` and read
   `Engine/ParcHost/overlay.json` on `origin/main`. Your version is main's plus
   one at the moment you publish, not when you started.
2. **Before publishing an engine change**, rebase onto `origin/main`, keep every
   hash main already records for files you didn't change, regenerate only your
   own, and set the version to main's plus one. Recheck right before `git push`;
   if main moved, rebase and renumber again.
3. **Before `overlay-check` or `overlay-apply`**, fetch and make sure the
   manifest you pass is current `origin/main`'s (or the exact commit you mean to
   build). Never apply a branch's manifest that main has already overtaken.
4. **Before starting an engine build**, confirm the applied overlay equals
   `origin/main`'s, so the build includes every engine change other sessions
   have landed.

On November 1, three pending branches claimed overlay 289 while another
session published 288 and a further engine change, and a fast-forward of the
main checkout nearly broke a packaging step that was waiting on an older
engine.
